    dict(key='22-class-2', kind='code', title='Bank — one primitive, two calls',
         body="// Bank: seals and opens with Tink's Aead\nthis.aead = keyset.getPrimitive(\n        RegistryConfiguration.get(), Aead.class);\n...\nreturn aead.encrypt(plaintext, associatedData);     // Tink picks the nonce\n...\nreturn aead.decrypt(sealed, associatedData);\n} catch (GeneralSecurityException e) {              // any change: refused\n    return null;",
         narration='The bank asks the keyset for an A E A D primitive. [[slnc 300]] Sealing is one call to encrypt, with the line and the account. [[slnc 300]] Opening is one call to decrypt. [[slnc 300]] If anything was changed, Tink throws, and the bank reports refused.'),
    dict(key='23-class-3', kind='code', title='DemoKeysets — a keyset with a known key and id',
         body='// DemoKeysets: fixed keysets, so every run prints the same\nString json = "{\\"primaryKeyId\\":" + keyId\n        + ",\\"key\\":[{\\"keyData\\":{\\"typeUrl\\":"\n...\nreturn CleartextKeysetHandle.read(\n        JsonKeysetReader.withString(json));',
         narration='Demo keysets builds a keyset from fixed key bytes, so the output repeats. [[slnc 300]] Real code calls generate new, which picks a random key.'),
    dict(key='24-class-4', kind='code', title='TinkLayout — prefix + nonce + AES-GCM',
         body='// TinkLayout: builds a Tink message by hand\nreturn ByteBuffer.allocate(PREFIX)\n        .put((byte) 1).putInt(keyId).array();     // version, key id\n...\nCipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding");\ncipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, new SecretKeySpec(key, "AES"),\n        new GCMParameterSpec(TAG * 8, nonce));\ncipher.updateAAD(associatedData);               // the account\n...\n.put(prefix(keyId)).put(nonce).put(cipherTextAndTag).array();',
         narration="Tink layout redoes act two by hand. [[slnc 300]] The prefix is the version one, then the key number as four bytes. [[slnc 300]] Then Java's own A E S G C M seals the line with the nonce and the account. [[slnc 300]] Prefix, nonce, cipher text and tag. [[slnc 300]] A test shows Tink opens it, and rebuilds a real Tink message byte for byte."),
    dict(key='25-class-5', kind='code', title='EnvelopeAead — encrypt: wrap, then seal',
         body='// EnvelopeAead: a fresh data key per message, wrapped by the master key\nbyte[] dataKey = new byte[32];\nRANDOM.nextBytes(dataKey);\nbyte[] wrapped = master.encrypt(dataKey, associatedData);  // 32 bytes\nbyte[] cipherText = aeadFor(dataKey)\n        .encrypt(plaintext, associatedData);\nreturn ByteBuffer.allocate(4 + wrapped.length + cipherText.length)\n        .putInt(wrapped.length).put(wrapped).put(cipherText).array();',
         narration='Envelope A E A D makes thirty two random bytes, a data key, for every message. [[slnc 300]] The master key wraps only that data key. [[slnc 300]] The data key seals the line. [[slnc 300]] And the stored message is the length, the wrapped key, and the cipher text.'),
